WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.215
Lesson 1.8.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:04.560
Privacy and physical security.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.517
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:06.517 --> 00:00:08.845
able to keep your holdings private and your

00:00:08.845 --> 00:00:11.225
household safe as your on-chain wealth grows.

00:00:12.525 --> 00:00:13.815
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.815 --> 00:00:15.500
First, the key ideas.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.215
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:18.215 --> 00:00:19.740
Then, your checklist.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.902
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:25.337
First, Blockchains are public.

00:00:25.337 --> 00:00:27.340
Anyone who links an address to you

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.987
can see its balance and history, forever.

00:00:29.987 --> 00:00:34.325
Next, Link less: don't post addresses, screenshots or wins

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:39.471
publicly; use separate addresses for public activity (e.g. donations,

00:00:39.471 --> 00:00:43.735
NFTs) and for savings; be aware that exchange withdrawals

00:00:43.735 --> 00:00:47.711
link your identity (KYC) to the receiving address.

00:00:47.711 --> 00:00:50.443
Then, Name services (e.g. an ENS name)

00:00:50.443 --> 00:00:53.245
are convenient but make an address easy

00:00:53.245 --> 00:00:55.716
to find and associate with you.

00:00:55.716 --> 00:00:58.910
Also, Physical risk ("wrench attacks"): criminals

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.446
target people known to hold crypto.

00:01:01.446 --> 00:01:03.821
Low profile is the first defence.

00:01:03.821 --> 00:01:07.027
Multisig with keys in separate locations means no

00:01:07.027 --> 00:01:10.341
single person can be forced to move everything.

00:01:10.341 --> 00:01:13.602
And finally, Home and devices: hardware wallets and

00:01:13.602 --> 00:01:17.052
backups out of sight; encrypted devices; don't discuss

00:01:17.052 --> 00:01:19.811
holdings with people who don't need to know.

00:01:21.112 --> 00:01:23.627
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:23.627 --> 00:01:25.328
Alex posts a screenshot of a

00:01:25.328 --> 00:01:27.622
big win, with the address visible.

00:01:27.622 --> 00:01:30.012
Anyone can now see Alex's balance and

00:01:30.012 --> 00:01:32.717
follow every future move, and scammers and

00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:34.652
thieves now have a target.

00:01:34.652 --> 00:01:38.099
Better: no screenshots, a vault address that has never

00:01:38.099 --> 00:01:41.107
been shared, and a 2-of-3 multisig so no single

00:01:41.107 --> 00:01:43.802
key holder can be coerced into draining it.

00:01:45.102 --> 00:01:46.562
Here's your checklist.

00:01:46.562 --> 00:01:48.097
Do it now, for real.

00:01:48.097 --> 00:01:50.373
First, Savings addresses have never

00:01:50.373 --> 00:01:52.632
been posted or linked publicly.

00:01:52.632 --> 00:01:56.697
Next, no holdings screenshots or wins on social media.

00:01:56.697 --> 00:01:58.397
Then, Vault keys held so no

00:01:58.397 --> 00:02:00.762
single person can move everything.

00:02:00.762 --> 00:02:03.356
And finally, Backups stored discreetly, away

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:05.027
from where people would look.

00:02:06.327 --> 00:02:07.537
Question 1.

00:02:07.537 --> 00:02:10.092
What does linking an address to your identity reveal?

00:02:14.512 --> 00:02:16.453
The answer: Its full balance and

00:02:16.453 --> 00:02:18.277
history, now and in the future.

00:02:19.577 --> 00:02:20.822
Question 2.

00:02:20.822 --> 00:02:23.452
How does multisig help against physical threats?

00:02:27.872 --> 00:02:30.432
The answer: No single person can be forced

00:02:30.432 --> 00:02:33.112
to move everything; other keys are elsewhere.

00:02:34.412 --> 00:02:35.682
Question 3.

00:02:35.682 --> 00:02:38.050
What links your identity to a wallet address?

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:46.037
The answer: For example, KYC exchange withdrawals,

00:02:46.037 --> 00:02:49.325
name services, or posting the address publicly.

00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:51.870
Let's recap.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.033
Blockchains are public: Anyone who links an address to

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.920
you can see its balance and history, forever.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.165
Link less: don't post addresses, screenshots or wins publicly.

00:03:02.165 --> 00:03:04.776
Name services (e.g. an ENS name) are

00:03:04.776 --> 00:03:07.529
convenient but make an address easy to

00:03:07.529 --> 00:03:09.805
find and associate with you.

00:03:09.805 --> 00:03:11.740
Physical risk ("wrench attacks").

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.555
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:15.555 --> 00:03:19.880
Next up, Lesson 1.9: Smart accounts and account abstraction.
